When you actually update the source (i.e. the stuff in
containers/web/src/ ), you need to:

$ cd ~/lensisku/containers/web/src/building/
$ ./build-npm.sh
$ ./build-rust.sh

Those scripts build from `Dockerfile.npm` (`node:24-alpine` +
Corepack/`packageManager` pin from `frontend/package.json`, currently
`pnpm@12.3.4`) and `Dockerfile.rust` (`rust:1.98.1-trixie` + pinned
`nightly-2026-09-29`, `mold`, and `rustc-codegen-cranelift-preview`).
The source tree requires that nightly (see `rust-toolchain.toml`);
`.cargo/config.toml` enables mold, `-Zthreads=8`, and Cranelift for
`dev`/`test` only. The Rust helper builds its image automatically and
defaults to the release-fast Cargo profile (opt-level 3, no cross-crate
LTO, 16 codegen units, incremental, LLVM — not Cranelift).

Rebuild the `build-rust` image after pulling source that changes
`building/Dockerfile.rust` (e.g. `podman rmi build-rust` then re-run
`./build-rust.sh`) so the builder picks up mold, Cranelift, and other
packages.

For maximum runtime optimization on the same nightly:

$ CARGO_BUILD_PROFILE=release ./build-rust.sh

(`release` keeps fat LTO + codegen-units=1 on LLVM. Stable toolchains are
not supported while `.cargo/config.toml` uses nightly-only flags.)

Compiled dependencies and incremental artifacts remain in the source checkout's
target/container/ directory across helper runs. The executable is copied to
target/release/lensisku, which both web and web-dev run from the /src bind mount.
Removing containers or rebuilding runtime images does not delete this cache.
Remove target/container/ explicitly if a clean build is needed.

The Trixie Rust builder matches the runtime's native libraries, including ICU 76.
Runtime web/web-dev images do not compile Rust and do not need `mold`.
Repeat these source build steps in containers/web-dev/src/building/ when updating
the dev deployment. LBCS build_image.sh/rebuild_images.sh only rebuild runtime
images; they do not compile the application. The root Lensisku Dockerfile is a
separate build path and is not used by this deployment.

We don't do this in a pre-script or something because it runs its
own specialized docker containers.
